# CHAPTER 4 — IMPLEMENTATION AND TESTING

## 4.1 Implementation Strategy

Cipher Labs will be developed incrementally to make the architecture easier to test and to reduce the risk of unsafe operations.

The implementation is divided into four phases. Each phase has its own deliverables and validation requirements. Features will be reported as completed only after the relevant implementation and tests provide supporting evidence.

## 4.2 Phase 0 — Environment and Application Foundation

Phase 0 establishes the development environment and application structure.

The planned tasks include:

1. Preparing the frontend and backend project structure.
2. Configuring the Tauri desktop application.
3. Establishing the FastAPI application entry point.
4. Implementing a backend health endpoint.
5. Configuring MongoDB Atlas connectivity without exposing credentials.
6. Configuring the desktop-sidecar integration and permissions.
7. Preparing the Windows packaging configuration.
8. Verifying configuration and startup behaviour.

**Expected outcome:** A reproducible application foundation with a working desktop-to-backend communication path and a documented configuration process.

Completion will be established through startup tests, health checks and verified build results.

## 4.3 Phase 1 — Authentication, Database and Simulation

Phase 1 introduces the core application workflows.

### 4.3.1 Authentication and Database Integration

The backend will implement the configured authentication mechanism and enforce access to user-specific records. MongoDB Atlas will store persistent application metadata and operation history.

The data model will use appropriate document structures, indexes and uniqueness constraints where required. Access restrictions will be implemented in the backend rather than relying on frontend navigation alone.

### 4.3.2 Dashboard Development

The dashboard will present the application's available metrics and distinguish simulations from eligible verification results. Unavailable features and unavailable database data will be represented explicitly.

### 4.3.3 Simulation Workflow

The simulation module will demonstrate the sequence of an erasure operation without changing real files. The application will record the simulated target and the results of its checks.

The report will explicitly state:

“This is a simulation. No physical erasure was performed. The check confirms only that the simulated targets were not changed.”

### 4.3.4 Integrity Checks

Hash-based comparison may be used to evaluate the consistency of test data. The report will identify the data being compared and the purpose of the comparison.

A matching hash will be interpreted as an integrity result, not as proof that all copies of a file have been erased.

### 4.3.5 Report Generation

The application will generate JSON and PDF reports from available operation metadata. The generated report must accurately reflect the recorded status and must not convert simulated results into actual sanitization claims.

**Expected outcome:** An initial application containing authentication, database-backed records, dashboard functionality, controlled simulation workflows and basic reporting, subject to successful implementation and testing.

## 4.4 Phase 2 — Test-Image Recovery and Machine Learning

Phase 2 will introduce controlled recovery experiments.

### 4.4.1 Test-Image Generation

The application will generate or use designated test images within its application-data directory. These images will contain known test content so that recovery results can be evaluated against expected outcomes.

### 4.4.2 File Identification

The recovery component will examine the approved test data for supported file signatures or other defined identification features.

Candidate detection will not be treated as conclusive evidence of successful recovery.

### 4.4.3 Recovery Validation

Recovery candidates will be checked using defined structural or integrity conditions. A candidate will be marked “Recovered” only after the required output has been written and re-validated.

### 4.4.4 Local Machine Learning

scikit-learn may be used to evaluate local classification techniques on generated test fixtures. The model's purpose, input features, dataset composition and limitations will be documented.

Model performance will be assessed using suitable metrics such as precision, recall and F1-score. These metrics will be calculated from test results rather than estimated or assumed.

**Expected outcome:** A controlled test-image recovery workflow and an experimentally evaluated local classification component, provided the relevant features are implemented successfully.

## 4.5 Phase 3 — Packaging and Reliability

Phase 3 focuses on deployment and reliability.

The planned activities include:

1. Packaging the Python backend using PyInstaller.
2. Configuring the Tauri sidecar integration.
3. Preparing a Windows installer using NSIS.
4. Implementing the limited metadata-only offline queue.
5. Testing idempotent synchronisation.
6. Testing retry and database-outage behaviour.
7. Checking that credentials are not embedded in frontend assets or logs.
8. Validating the installer and application on a clean Windows test environment.

**Expected outcome:** A deployable Windows application with tested startup, packaging, error handling and synchronisation behaviour.

## 4.6 Testing and Validation Strategy

Testing will be performed at several levels.

### 4.6.1 Unit Testing

Unit tests will evaluate individual functions, including input validation, status assignment, hash comparison, report formatting and recovery-validation rules.

### 4.6.2 Integration Testing

Integration tests will verify communication between the desktop application, backend API and MongoDB Atlas. Tests will include successful requests, invalid requests and database-unavailable scenarios.

### 4.6.3 Functional Testing

Functional testing will examine login, dashboard navigation, simulation execution, verification results, report generation and other implemented workflows.

### 4.6.4 Security Testing

Security testing will assess access-control enforcement, input validation, secret handling, restricted process permissions and the application's handling of user-specific records.

### 4.6.5 Recovery Testing

Recovery tests will use generated test files and application-created test images. Known inputs and expected results will be used to evaluate candidate identification and output validation.

### 4.6.6 Packaging Testing

Packaging tests will verify that the application launches correctly from the generated Windows installer and that the backend sidecar is available when required.

