# CHAPTER 5 — CONCLUSION AND FUTURE ENHANCEMENTS

## 5.1 Conclusion

Cipher Labs is proposed as a Windows desktop application that brings together data-sanitization simulations, controlled file-recovery experiments, verification checks and audit-report generation.

The project addresses the need for transparent status reporting and careful interpretation of technical evidence. Its design separates operation execution, validation and reporting so that users can understand what was performed and what the resulting evidence supports.

The proposed architecture combines Tauri, React, TypeScript, FastAPI and MongoDB Atlas. A phased development process is intended to establish the application foundation before introducing simulation workflows, test-image recovery, machine-learning experiments and Windows packaging.

A central design principle is that the application must not claim that a simulation proves physical erasure. Likewise, detected recovery candidates must not be presented as successfully recovered files until the defined validation requirements have been satisfied.

The project's progress and final effectiveness will depend on successful implementation, documented test results and evidence that the expected behaviour has been achieved. The report therefore distinguishes the proposed design from functionality that has actually been implemented and verified.

## 5.2 Future Enhancements

Potential future enhancements include:

1. Extending the recovery module to support additional file formats within controlled test environments.
2. Improving recovery-candidate validation and classification using experimentally evaluated machine-learning models.
3. Adding richer audit-report filtering and export capabilities.
4. Improving usability and accessibility across the desktop interface.
5. Expanding automated integration and reliability testing.
6. Evaluating additional authentication options after the required integrations have been configured and tested.
7. Conducting a separate technical and safety review before considering any real-device sanitization functionality.
8. Evaluating standards-aligned sanitization methods and appropriate independent verification procedures before making claims about physical media.

Any future expansion into real-device operations must undergo separate design, safety assessment and validation. It is not included in the current implementation scope.

